{"jsonrpc":"2.0","t":343523,"method":"harness","params":{"epoch":"ac5e80358f04","items":[{"anchor":{"bytes":6978178,"epoch":"f5362ae336a917cf","line":18,"needle":"┃"},"at":"2026-11-09T14:02:30.897Z","id":"msg_1c09d3aa3001olslNyfPbKfCP0-r0","kind":"thinking","seq":112,"thinking":{"partial":true,"text":"Failure: the first incident found (newest) has index 10267, and the next one is 10697? Wait, expected value 10697 < 10267 failed — meaning the second incident's datetime appears at a position later than the first? No wait: last starts as Infinity. First iteration: idx = position of the newest incident, must be less than Infinity, pass, last = idx. Second iteration: idx of the second-newest must be less than last (the position of the newest) — failed. That is, the second-newest datetime appears later in the document than the newest one.\n\nWhy? The data file isn't sorted (latest is last). The loader sorts. But wait — the test's `loadIncidents()` returns a sorted array. The app also sorts. So the timeline should be in newest-first order...\n\nMaybe: the second-newest `datetime=\"2026-11-07T22:05:00Z\"` — hmm. Oh! The issue is: the first datetime found via indexOf isn't necessarily inside the timeline. `datetime=\"2026-11-09T13:55:00Z\"`... no, there's no other occurrence.\n\nWait, maybe the issue is this: two incidents... let me think. Order of the sorted array: 11-09T13:55, 11-07T22:05, 10-28T16:40, 10-14T08:12. indexOf for each item:\n\n- 11-09: position X1\n- 11-07: position X2 > X1 → failure.\n\nHmm, why is the 11-07 datetime later in the document than the 11-09 one? The timeline is rendered with `incidents.map(renderIncident).join(\"\")` — the order of the sorted array... the sort compares `Date.parse(b.time) - Date.parse(a.time)` — descending. That's correct.\n\nWait — maybe the issue is the first `last` check: `expect(idx).toBeGreaterThan(-1)` — no problem. Hmm, let me actually check: does the string `datetime=\"2026-11-09T13:55:00Z\"` appear earlier? No.\n\nHold on — the error is \"expected 10697 to be less than 10267\". So the first incident's idx = 10267, the second = 10697. The second-newest (11-07) appears after the newest (11-09)? That would mean the rendering is in oldest-first order. So the sort didn't happen?! But the /api/incidents test that expects toEqual(loadIncidents()) passes — trivially, since both call loadIncidents.\n\nDid loadIncidents really sort? `[...incidents].sort((a, b) => Date.parse(b.time) - Date.parse(a.time))`. For b = 11-09, a = 10-14: parse(b) - parse(a) = positive → b comes after a → correct descending order.\n\nHmm, wait, maybe the issue is that `res.text` contains the datetime somewhere else... no, actually! Maybe the first datetime found via indexOf isn't inside the timeline. `datetime=\"2026-11-09T13:55:00Z\"`... no, there's no other occurrence.\n\nWait, maybe I should check: is the timeline even being rendered in the first place? `renderIncidents` — the test \"colours each incident by severity\" passes, so the li element exists.\n\nLet me debug: print the positions. Actually, wait — maybe I got it. The failure is in the test, and the app's timeline is in oldest-first order? Let me re-examine the sort in my head with the actual data:\n\nData file order: 10-14, 10-28, 11-07, 11-09.\nSort comparison (a,b): Date.parse(b.time) - Date.parse(a.time).\nArray is already in ascending time order. For adjacent pair a=10-14, b=10-28: b - a > 0 → swap? In Array.sort, if the comparator returns positive, a is sorted after b. b - a > 0 → a (10-14) goes after b (10-28). So ascending input → descending output. Correct: 11-09, 11-07, 10-28, 10-14.\n\nSo the timeline order should be newest first. Then why does indexOf show 11-07 after 11-09?\n\nOh, wait. I think I get it. `res.text.indexOf(\"datetime=\\\"2026-11-09T13:55:00Z\\\"\")` — but does 11-09's datetime appear anywhere else? No...\n\nHmm, let me actually reconsider: the first failure was \"expected 10697 to be less than 10267\" at line 37. Line 37 is `expect(idx).toBeLessThan(last)`. So last = 10267 (the previous idx), current idx = 10697. Which iteration? Could be the second (11-07), or another one.\n\nHmm wait, maybe the issue is: the newest incident 11-09T13:55 appears at 10267, but where? Maybe not in the timeline but somewhere else... the updated banner? `Updated 9 Nov 2026, 14:20 UTC` — no datetime attribute there.\n\nActually, let me just debug by fetching and inspecting.\n\nActually wait. Got it. The test iterates over `loadIncidents()` — sorted. But `res.text.indexOf(\"datetime=\\\"...\\\"\")` — the first match for `datetime=\"2026-11-07T22:05:00Z\"` is... each time string only appears once. So positions must reflect the document order of the <time> elements.\n\nCould it be... the sort isn't actually descending! Let me re-check my comparator: `(a, b) => Date.parse(b.time) - Date.parse(a.time)`. Comparator returns >0 means a comes after b. b.time=11-09, a.time=10-14 → positive → a (10-14) goes after b (11-09). Result: 11-09 first. Descending. Correct.\n\nHmm, so why? Let me actually run a quick node check against the rendered output.\n\nActually — wait. The test \"renders every incident under the services, newest first\" — the count check `expect(res.text.match(/class=\"incident /g)).toHaveLength(incidents.length)` — did it pass? The failure is at line 37, so the count passed (line 32), the timeline index also passed.\n\nHmm, hold on — `/class=\"incident /g` also matches `class=\"incident-sev\"` and `class=\"incident-note\"`! \"incident-sev\" starts with \"incident \" — no wait: `class=\"incident ` — \"incident-sev\" has a hyphen after \"incident\", not a space. So no match. OK.\n\nSo positions: 11-09 at 10267, 11-07 at 10697? That's weird. Unless the first occurrence of the `datetime=\"2026-11-09T13:55:00Z\"` string is at 10267 and the second occurrence of 11-07 is... hmm.\n\nLet me actually debug — run node, fetch the page, print the indices.\n"},"turnId":"turn-msg_1c098fc65001qgMZbDNnPZwXXi"}],"kind":"